// cffi U2 — THE WIDTH PAIR, ARM 1 (`-> i32`).  Sibling: ffiw-i64.mid.
//
// The two fixtures differ in EXACTLY ONE character of meaning: the DECLARED
// RESULT WIDTH of the same libc call on the same argument.  Nothing else —
// same module shape, same `access` call, same nonexistent path, same
// `mkbool(r < 0)`.
//
// WHAT IS BEING MEASURED (cffi plan §7.1/§7.2, MEASURED on this host's
// glibc 2.44): `access`'s own body returns its failure with
// `movl $-1, %eax`, so the upper 32 bits of %rax are left ZERO —
// the raw `l` value is 4294967295, not -1.  A `rawcall` (all-word, the
// pre-U2 shape) reads exactly that, so `r < 0` is FALSE and the runtime
// SILENTLY SKIPS its error branch.
//
//   THIS ARM (`-> i32`, sign)      -> `=w call $access` + `extsw` -> true
//   THE SIBLING (`-> i64`, no ext) -> `=l call $access`          -> false
//
// Why the pair and not either arm alone: a lowering that IGNORES the
// declared width prints false for both; one that extends UNCONDITIONALLY
// prints true for both.  Neither can pass the pair by accident, and both
// arms are goldens.  The i64 arm is also the honest legacy control — it is
// exactly what `rawcall` did before this unit.
//
// WHY `access` AND NOT `openat`/`getpid`/`poll`/`waitpid`/`readdir`: those
// five are PRE-EXTENDED on this glibc (measured), so an oracle built on any
// of them would be a FALSE NEGATIVE — it would pass before the fix too.
module Ffwi32
export Ffwi32.neg : val -> val

extern access(path : ptr, mode : i32) -> i32;

defun Ffwi32.neg(dummy : val) -> val {
  word r = rawcall access(rawstr("/no-such-file-ffiw-xyzzy"), 0);
  return mkbool(r < 0);
}
